// The scattering — a slide where food has to be *found*.
//
// Every other world in the library ends the same way: the autotroph divides until the slide is
// an even mat at carrying capacity, and from then on a cell that eats other cells is always
// already touching one. There is no search problem, so there is nothing for a sense to be for,
// and a photosensor is upkeep with no return. That is why `docs/ECONOMY.md` finds the autotroph
// winning everything, and it is why no route in `benches/routes.rs` could hold a photosensor:
// seeing costs, and on a mat it buys nothing.
//
// This world is sparse instead. At 232 lux an ancestor settles at about **190 cells on 65,536
// squares** — one per 345 squares, a mean spacing near nineteen. A photosensor's default reach
// is six squares, so a cell here can see essentially nothing, and a hunter that stands still
// starves beside a slide that has food on it.
//
// # Why the limit is light, and not any of the obvious things
//
// Measured on this slide, each run 60,000 ticks with `ancestor.mm` from 64 founders:
//
//   space            `split_pressure` alone settles about 15,000 — a mat
//   carbon 8,192     16,591 at tick 6,000, then **373** at 30,000 — a collapse, not a cap
//   CO2 ÷32          15,497. Cutting it thirty-two-fold barely moves the population at all
//   light 512        15,047 — still a mat
//   light 232        **190, and flat at 20k, 40k and 60k**
//   light 176        2
//
// The CO2 row explains the others. **A recycled resource cannot cap a population**: respiration
// hands carbon dioxide straight back, so a smaller pool simply cycles faster and supports the
// same crowd. Only something that does not come back can set a ceiling.
//
// Structural carbon is the one resource that does not come back — which is why leaning on it
// produces a collapse rather than a cap. Bodies are built from chemical 4 alone; half of every
// corpse becomes carrion, carrion decays to carbon dioxide, and a lysosome digests it to sugar.
// Nothing in the default chemistry produces chemical 4. Over the carbon-lean run above, carbon
// fell from 552M to 25M while sugar rose to 11.1 *billion*: the population starved for something
// to build with while sitting on four hundred times its remaining carbon in a form it cannot
// build with.
//
// Light is the only limit left that is neither recycled nor consumed. It is re-prescribed every
// fluid step, so it caps the *rate* of production without a stock to draw down, and a population
// held by it sits still for as long as you care to watch.
//
// The slide is 256 squares because that is what the microscope opens on, and because a search
// problem needs somewhere to search. On the 64-square worlds most of the library uses, a
// photosensor at the default six-square range already sees an eighth of the width.
//
// # Two things to know before trusting it
//
// **The band is narrow.** 320 lux gives 18,872 cells and 176 gives 2. The usable range is about
// 224 to 240, and this world sits close enough to the cliff that adding predators may push it
// over rather than establish an ecology. That is not a tuning failure to be dialled out; it is
// what a world with no structural recycling has instead of a carrying capacity.
//
// **It is stable against the founder, not against evolution.** At 256 lux the population held at
// 202 for twenty thousand ticks and then a mutant lineage escaped the light limit — seven
// species and 8,999 cells by tick 60,000, the mat returning by another road. At 232 it stays one
// species. Treat a long run here as an experiment with an outcome, not as a fixed stage.
//
//   mm-cli run scenarios/the_scattering.ron --genome genomes/evader.mm --ticks 60000
(
    name: "the scattering",
    seed: 20260814,
    width: 256,
    height: 256,

    // Dim, and uniform. Dim is the whole scenario; uniform is so that a cell cannot find the
    // crowded places by phototaxis instead of by looking at other cells — ambient light is a
    // field lookup with no range limit at all, and a gradient would be a cheaper cue than the
    // one this world exists to make valuable.
    light: Uniform(intensity: 232),
    current: Still,
    fluid_interval: 1,

    // Everything else in full measure. Scarcity here is how fast the light lets a cell work,
    // not anything running out — see the carbon row above for what running out looks like.
    seeding: [
        Uniform(chemical: 11, per_square: 409600),
        // The carbonate system (`docs/CHEMISTRY.md` §11). Carbonate matched to the dissolved
        // CO2 above, so the slide starts at pH 7 and every move from there is something the
        // cells did. Calcium is then *not* free: the pair precipitates on the product of the
        // two, so this is set to put `sqrt(calcium * carbonate)` a little over
        // `minerals.calcite_saturation` — near enough to equilibrium that the pH decides which
        // way it goes, which is the whole point of the coupling.
        Uniform(chemical: 18, per_square: 409600),
        Uniform(chemical: 17, per_square: 3461),
        Uniform(chemical: 14, per_square: 409600),
        Uniform(chemical: 4, per_square: 409600),
        // The three minerals nothing in the engine produces (`docs/CHEMISTRY.md` §8): seeding is
        // the only way in, and without them a nucleus, an enzyme or a shell cannot be built at
        // all. At the Redfield proportion of this world's own carbon — 16/106 nitrogen, 1/106
        // phosphorus rounded up — and half that again in silicon, matching the fresh slide.
        Uniform(chemical: 5, per_square: 61440),
        Uniform(chemical: 6, per_square: 4096),
        // The inert pool a diazosome cracks. Four times the bioavailable nitrogen: a
        // young world is mostly locked up, and unlocking it is what the organelle is for.
        Uniform(chemical: 16, per_square: 245760),
        // Silicon, which is what a shell is made of and which nothing produces.
        //
        // A world has only the silicon it is seeded with, so whether armour is *possible* is a
        // property of the slide rather than of the catalogue — and most of the library has none,
        // which means a mutant that builds a shell there simply cannot afford it. That is
        // deliberate here and worth knowing about elsewhere: this is the world meant to have a
        // predator in it, so it is the world that should let its prey answer.
        //
        // A twentieth of the carbon. Armour is contested only by whatever is armoured, so it does
        // not need to be plentiful to be worth having — and being scarce is what makes it a
        // second axis of competition rather than a second tax on everybody.
        Uniform(chemical: 7, per_square: 20480),
    ],
    barriers: [],
)
